 | What                                         | Why, and what happens instead                                                                                                                                                                                                    |
 | -------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
 | Objects are not freed at the last `del`      | There are no reference counts. Finalizers and weak reference callbacks run at the first safe point after the collection that finds the object dead; `gc.collect()` forces it; everything left is finalized at exit. See README.   |
-| Buffered output reaches files earlier        | `open(path, "w").write(text)` counts on the file being closed when it is dropped. Here the streams over files that hold output are kept in a list, and are written out before the program, or anything it starts, can look at a file: `open`, `stat`, starting a process, `gc.collect()`, a call of the host. So `f.write("abc"); os.stat(path).st_size` is 3, not 0. The descriptor itself stays open until a collection finds the file. |
+| Buffered output reaches files earlier        | `open(path, "w").write(text)` counts on the file being closed when it is dropped. Here the streams over files that hold output are kept in a list, and are written out before the program, or anything it starts, can look at a file or do to a file or a descriptor what would come out differently afterwards: `open`, the `stat` family and `DirEntry.stat`, reading, writing, seeking, truncating and closing through `os` or a `FileIO`, `dup2`, `utime`, `rename`, `link`, starting a process, `gc.collect()`, a call of the host. So `f.write("abc"); os.stat(path).st_size` is 3, not 0. Only streams that are exactly what `open()` makes (`TextIOWrapper`, `BufferedWriter`, `BufferedRandom` over a `FileIO`, none of a derived class, whose `write` and `flush` would be seen to run), and not `sys.stdout` and `sys.stderr`. What tells another process by other means that a file is ready (a socket, a signal) does not write them out. The descriptor itself stays open until a collection finds the file: the reader of a pipe whose writer was dropped gets the data, but no end of file until then. |
 | `sys.getrefcount`, `gc.collect()`'s result   | There are no counts: `getrefcount` returns a constant, `gc.collect()` returns 0. `gc.get_objects()`, `get_referrers()` and `get_referents()` are real, and each costs a full collection that is listened to. |
 | `__del__` given to a class after instances exist | Instances register for finalization when they are made.                                                                                                                                                                       |
 | All NaNs are one object                      | A NaN is an immediate value: `float('nan') is float('nan')`, so `x in [x]`-style identity shortcuts see any NaN as the same. The sign and payload of a NaN are not kept.                                                          |
@@ -50,7 +50,8 @@ stays), **experiment** (needs trying out before it can be decided), **deferred**
 | Python importing JS                       | Done, see README. Missing: `import a.b` for a subpath of a JS package (use `importlib.import_module("a/b")`), modules with top-level await (`require()` refuses them), the name of the module in `ImportError` messages. |
 | Output of two languages                   | `console.*` first writes what `print()` has left in the buffers of `sys.stdout` and `sys.stderr`, so the order is kept when they are pipes or files. `process.stdout.write()` and `Bun.write(Bun.stdout, ...)` do not. |
 | Iterators of JS                           | `for` and `async for` in Python take items from a JS iterator as they go. Leaving the loop early does not call the iterator's `return()`. |
-| Too many open files                       | A system call that fails with `EMFILE` collects, finalizes and tries again (`socket`, `accept`, `socketpair`, `pipe`, `dup`, `os.open`, `opendir`). Other limits that dropped objects hold on to (memory maps, locks on files, child processes) are not treated so. |
+| `OrderedDict` behind its back             | `collections.OrderedDict` is native and has no list beside the dict: its order is the order of the dict's entries, which can be taken from and added to at both ends. `dict.__delitem__(od, key)`, `dict.pop(od, key)` and `dict.popitem(od)`, after which CPython's raises `KeyError` from `repr` and iteration, do here what the methods of `od` do. |
+| Too many open files                       | A system call that fails with `EMFILE` collects, finalizes and tries again (`open`, `os.open`, `socket`, `accept`, `socketpair`, `pipe`, `dup`, `opendir`). Other limits that dropped objects hold on to (memory maps, locks on files, child processes) are not treated so. |
 | Exception scope verification              | `BUN_JSC_validateExceptionChecks=1` (Bun's ASAN CI sets it) fails as soon as the runtime starts. The C++ of the runtime has to be gone through.                            |
 | Signals                                   | Handlers run at safe points (`VMTraps::NeedPythonCalls`), where a system call is interrupted, and between turns of the host's event loop. Not while JS code runs. `SIGPWR` is the engine's. No Windows. |
 | Tracing and profiling                     | `sys.settrace`, `sys.setprofile`: stored, never called. `sys.monitoring` has its whole API and takes registrations, but no event ever fires. So no `pdb` (`python3 -m pdb x.py` never stops, and runs the program again and again), no `bdb`, no `coverage`, no `trace`. `cProfile` loads and reports nothing. |
diff --git a/Source/JavaScriptCore/python/README.md b/Source/JavaScriptCore/python/README.md
index a9cfbdbd..699785ad 100644
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/python/README.md
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/python/README.md
@@ -49,6 +49,15 @@ handed to JS (the result of a call, an argument, an attribute, an operator's res
 (`valueForJS`), so that floatness is lost in JS and nothing else is. Inside a list, which is shared storage, JS can see the cell;
 it behaves as a `Number` object does.
 
+**str.** A `JSString`, so UTF-16 where it is not Latin-1, while Python counts code points. A text without surrogate pairs is indexed
+directly. For one with pairs, `StrIndexCache` (16 entries for each runtime, emptied after each collection, nothing for texts shorter
+than 48 units) remembers the number of pairs and the offset of every 64th code point, so that `s[i]` in a loop does not scan from
+the start each time.
+
+**tuple, dict.** A tuple remembers its hash once it has been asked for. A dict keeps its entries in an array in insertion order, with
+an index beside it; it knows where its first entry is, and can leave room before it, so that taking and adding entries is as cheap at
+the front as at the back. `collections.OrderedDict` (`PyOrderedDict.cpp`) is that and nothing more.
+
 ## Code
 
 The compiler emits ordinary JSC bytecode where the semantics agree (moves, jumps, calls, exception handlers, generators through
